/**
 * @file isolation.dox
 * @brief Repository feature-isolation overview.
 *
 * @page feature_isolation Feature Isolation Analysis
 * @tableofcontents
 *
 * @section iso_purpose Purpose
 *
 * The isolation module searches for system witnesses that isolate a target
 * derived feature, or a remaining candidate feature set, relative to the other
 * available valid systems of an SPL.
 *
 * @section isolation_nav_sec Navigation and related pages
 *
 * Recommended reading order:
 *
 * - @ref documentation_guide
 * - @ref spl_parser
 * - @ref feature_analysis
 * - @ref feature_isolation
 *
 * Related pages:
 *
 * - @ref spl_examples_guide
 * - @ref fi_mathhelper

 * @section iso_theory_bridge Theory-to-implementation bridge
 *
 * In `main.pdf`, feature location is described with `Loc(t)` over atoms and a
 * system-to-atoms mapping. The repository does not construct that atom layer.
 * Its executable isolation semantics are instead expressed directly in the
 * derived-feature ID space built by `feature_analysis_t`.
 *
 * This page therefore documents the implementation's operationalization of the
 * paper idea, not a second independent theory.
 *
 * @section iso_core Core operational model
 *
 * For a target feature ID, `feature_isolation_t` partitions the available valid
 * systems into:
 *
 * - `I`: systems in which the target derived feature is present,
 * - `U`: systems in which the target derived feature is absent.
 *
 * It then computes:
 *
 * - `flatten_by_intersection(I)` — the features common to all systems in `I`,
 * - `flatten_by_union(U)` — the features appearing in at least one system in
 *   `U`,
 * - `flattened_difference(I, U)` — the candidate features isolated by the
 *   witness pair.
 *
 * The module can return either one witness (`some_difference`) or search the
 * minimal witness family (`minimal_differences`).
 *
 * @section iso_results Result model
 *
 * `difference_t` stores one witness pair `(I, U)`.
 *
 * `isolation_result_t` stores:
 *
 * - `features` — the isolated candidate feature set,
 * - `differences` — the witness pairs that isolate exactly that set.
 *
 * When the isolated feature set has size one, the visible report labels the
 * result `UNIQUE`. Larger isolated sets are reported as `AMBIGUOUS`, which is
 * especially relevant when some valid systems are marked missing.
 *
 * @section iso_search Search strategy
 *
 * The active implementation uses a dynamic witness search over available
 * systems and feature IDs. Internal helpers such as
 * `build_witness_candidates`, `select_branch_feature`, and
 * `search_minimal_differences` build and prune the witness search space.
 *
 * The current implementation no longer depends on a fixed compile-time bitset
 * width for witness search.
 *
 * @section iso_api Public API
 *
 * `feature_isolation_t` is the main entry point. Its public operations include:
 *
 * - `partition(feature_id)`
 * - `sid_to_fids(system_id)`
 * - `flatten_by_intersection(sids)`
 * - `flatten_by_union(sids)`
 * - `flattened_difference(I, U)`
 * - `minimal_differences(feature_id)`
 * - `some_difference(feature_id)`
 * - `print(...)`
 * - `name(feature_id)`
 *
 * The class owns the parsed SPL specification, the resolved report request,
 * and the derived feature-analysis object used by the search.
 *
 * @section iso_limits Limits and cost model
 *
 * Isolation runs on top of the finite derived feature registry. This keeps the
 * semantics explicit and inspectable, but the cost still grows with the size of
 * the valid system space and the number of derived features. Large SPLs may
 * therefore incur substantial report and isolation costs.
 */
